    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make something of the panel’s gender makeup, nor is it a reliable sign of anything amiss.** \n\nFocusing on the fact that the panel was composed entirely of women is unlikely to yield useful insights, and bringing it up with the consultancy would almost certainly be counterproductive. \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of why that is, what factors more likely drove the decision, and how you should handle the situation moving forward.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the Panel Makeup Isn't a Meaningful Signal\n* **Gender neutrality between finalists:** Both you and the successful candidate are women. An all-female panel choosing between female candidates provides no inherent basis for gender bias claims.\n* **Organizational demographics and committee scheduling:** Panels are typically assembled based on availability, specific functional roles (e.g., HR lead, department director, managing partner, external advisor), or deliberate internal policies to ensure women are represented on hiring committees. Sometimes scheduling and role assignments simply result in an all-female panel.\n* **Risk of misdirected focus:** If you raise the composition of the panel, it is likely to be viewed as grasping for grievance rather than seeking genuine professional feedback, potentially damaging your professional reputation in a relatively small industry.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What More Likely Influenced the Decision\nFor a **Head of Group** role at an engineering consultancy, selection criteria shift significantly away from pure technical seniority toward commercial and leadership capabilities:\n\n* **Commercial acumen and business development:** The announcement specifically cited **\"excellent client relationships.\"** At the head-of-group level, bringing in fees, winning tenders, and maintaining key accounts are often weighted just as heavily (or more heavily) than years of design experience.\n* **Internal familiarity:** Was the successful candidate an internal applicant? Internal candidates often have an advantage because their client relationships, work style, and rapport with firm leadership are already proven quantities.\n* **Vision and management style:** In final-round interviews for department heads, panels heavily evaluate communication, leadership philosophy, alignment with company strategy, and how well the candidate's vision matches where executive leadership wants to take the group.\n* **Diminishing returns on technical years:** Once a candidate meets the threshold of being chartered and having substantial project experience (which 8 years typically covers), having 15 years versus 8 years often matters less than commercial track record and team fit.\n\n---\n\n### 3. How to Interpret the Lack of Response (Two Weeks)\nTwo weeks without a reply to a request for written feedback is frustrating, but quite common:\n* **HR caution:** Many firms have strict policies limiting or vetting written post-interview feedback to minimize legal exposure.\n* **Workload and scheduling:** Panel members and HR personnel often have competing billable project priorities, travel, or leave.\n* **Drafting delay:** Constructing formal, constructive feedback across four interviewers often takes time to consolidate.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite, forward-looking follow-up:**\n   After two weeks, a brief, professional nudge is standard practice. Frame it around your personal growth rather than challenging their choice:\n   > *\"I hope you are having a productive week. Following up on my note from two weeks ago, I would still greatly appreciate any high-level feedback the panel might have regarding my interviews for the Head of Structural Engineering position. As I continue to pursue leadership opportunities, insights on areas where I can strengthen my profile would be invaluable.\"*\n\n2. **Offer an informal debrief call:**\n   People are often much more candid over a 10-minute phone call than in writing. Consider adding:\n   > *\"I would also be very glad to have a brief 10-minute phone conversation if that is more convenient than a written response.\"*\n\n3. **Reflect on the commercial angle:**\n   Given the rationale stated in the announcement, consider how prominent business development, client acquisition, and fee-earning strategies were in your interview presentation. Bolstering that aspect of your narrative will serve you well for future leadership positions.\n",
